UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 7, Lesson 2: Seasons and calendars of First Nations Peoples', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Many First Nations have seasonal calendars based on local signs such as flowering plants, animal behaviour and weather, rather than the four fixed seasons used in many school calendars. The number and names of seasons differ between Nations, so calendars must come from community-approved sources and are not interchangeable.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA student says \"All Aboriginal calendars have four seasons\". What should they do to correct this, and what kind of source should they use?\n\nFEEDBACK\nLook at community-approved published calendars from different Nations, such as those shared by First Nations organisations, and note that the number and names of seasons differ.\n\nWATCH FOR\nPainting a local calendar and calling it an Aboriginal calendar claims knowledge that belongs to Custodians.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCreate your own signs-of-the-year calendar for your area using what you can observe, such as flowering trees, birds and weather, across at least four months. Label it clearly as your own. List one published First Nations calendar source and one difference from yours.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Many First Nations have seasonal calendars based on local signs such as flowering plants, animal behaviour and weather, rather than the four fixed seasons used in many school calendars. The number and names of seasons differ between Nations, so calendars must come from community-approved sources and are not interchangeable.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-ACH-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Create your own signs-of-the-year calendar for your area using what you can observe, such as flowering trees, birds and weather, across at least four months. Label it clearly as your own. List one published First Nations Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Example: a class looks at a community-approved seasonal calendar published by a First Nations organisation. They record how the calendar uses signs such as the flowering of a plant to mark a change, then compare the number of seasons with the four-season calendar on the classroom wall.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Painting a local calendar and calling it an Aboriginal calendar claims knowledge that belongs to Custodians. Correction: Look at community-approved published calendars from different Nations, such as those shared by First Nations organisations, and note that the number and names of seasons differ.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Painting a local calendar and calling it an Aboriginal calendar claims knowledge that belongs to Custodians. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Many First Nations have seasonal calendars based on local signs such as flowering plants, animal behaviour and weather, rather than the four fixed seasons used in many school calendars. The number and names of seasons differ between Nations, so calendars must come from community-approved sources and are not interchangeable. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Many First Nations have seasonal calendars based on local signs such as flowering plants, animal behaviour and weather, rather than the four fixed seasons used in many school calendars. The number and names of seasons differ between Nations, so calendars must come from community-approved sources and are not interchangeable.","Painting a local calendar and calling it an Aboriginal calendar claims knowledge that belongs to Custodians.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the evidence\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Painting a local calendar and calling it an Aboriginal calendar claims knowledge that belongs to Custodians.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Skip respectful protocols because the first idea looked neat."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Skip respectful protocols because the first idea looked neat.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new inquiry\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Look at community-approved published calendars from different Nations, such as those shared by First Nations organisations, and note that the number and names of seasons differ. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["Look at community-approved published calendars from different Nations, such as those shared by First Nations organisations, and note that the number and names of seasons differ.","Painting a local calendar and calling it an Aboriginal calendar claims knowledge that belongs to Custodians.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"A student says \"All Aboriginal calendars have four seasons\". What should they do\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"List the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Look at community-approved published calendars from different Nations, such as those shared by First Nations organisations, and note that the number and names of seasons differ. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["Look at community-approved published calendars from different Nations, such as those shared by First Nations organisations, and note that the number and names of seasons differ.","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Painting a local calendar and calling it an Aboriginal calendar claims knowledge that belongs to Custodians.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with e\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["The new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Inquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant evidence with accurate reasoning and named sources or places.","question":"Inquire into or describe a fresh example that uses the central skill. Show evidence and name the sources or places that matter.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: Example: a class looks at a community-approved seasonal calendar published by a First Nations organisation. They record how the calendar uses signs such as the flowering of a plant to mark a change, then compare the number of seasons with the four-season calendar on the classroom wall.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change the claim or evidence, not just make the notes longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus corrected claim and an evidence-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely misconception for this HSIE topic, correct it and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Painting a local calendar and calling it an Aboriginal calendar claims knowledge that belongs to Custodians. Correction: Look at community-approved published calendars from different Nations, such as those shared by First Nations organisations, and note that the number and names of seasons differ.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w07-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise season.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"First Nations Histories and Cultures","success_criteria":["I can use season with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify calendar and explain why it matters.","I can practise sign independently on a new prompt.","I can notice local needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Many First Nations have seasonal calendars based on local signs such as flowering plants, animal behaviour and weather, rather than the four fixed seasons used in many school calendars. The number and names of seasons differ between Nations, so calendars must come from community-approved sources and are not interchangeable. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["Many First Nations have seasonal calendars based on local signs such as flowering plants, animal behaviour and weather, rather than the four fixed seasons used in many school calendars. The number and names of seasons differ between Nations, so calendars must come from community-approved sources and are not interchangeable.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a class looks at a community-approved seasonal calendar published by a First Nations organisation. They record how the calendar uses signs such as the flowering of a plant to mark a change, then compare the number of seasons with the four-season calendar on the classroom wall.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA student says \"All Aboriginal calendars have four seasons\". What should they do to correct this, and what kind of source should they use?\n\nFEEDBACK\nLook at community-approved published calendars from different Nations, such as those shared by First Nations organisations, and note that the number and names of seasons differ.","explain":"Many First Nations have seasonal calendars based on local signs such as flowering plants, animal behaviour and weather, rather than the four fixed seasons used in many school calendars. The number and names of seasons differ between Nations, so calendars must come from community-approved sources and are not interchangeable.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Painting a local calendar and calling it an Aboriginal calendar claims knowledge that belongs to Custodians. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Fixed seasons beside signs-based calendars.","caption":"Calendars differ between Nations.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Four seasons</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">one school calendar</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Local signs</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">plants, animals, weather</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example: a class looks at a community-approved seasonal calendar published by a First Nations organisation. They record  Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or c